{"id":"n-20261010-s43ym9","category":"finance","display_title":"Europe Faces Escalating Debt and Economic Strain","display_title_he":"אירופה ניצבת בפני חובות גואים ומצוקה כלכלית","short_summary":"Surging government bond yields, sluggish growth, and soaring defense obligations are straining European economies. France and Germany face significant fiscal and structural challenges amid political uncertainty.","english_level":"C1","words":461,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Tiny_zircon_grain_with_pacman_and_ghost_relicts.jpg/960px-Tiny_zircon_grain_with_pacman_and_ghost_relicts.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Machevariani · CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiny_zircon_grain_with_pacman_and_ghost_relicts.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"Walla","domain":"finance.walla.co.il","url":"https://finance.walla.co.il/item/3871397","title":"לא רק צרפת: אירופה שוקעת","published_at":"2026-10-10T08:49:00.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-10T08:49:00.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-10T06:05:21.760Z","quality_score":8,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Across the European continent, long celebrated for robust social safety nets and democratic stability, economic strains are intensifying. While Europe remains exceptionally wealthy, it increasingly lags behind the United States in economic expansion and trails both America and China in technological innovation, particularly within artificial intelligence. Compounding these structural headwinds, a dramatic surge in government borrowing costs and geopolitical pressures is destabilizing the continent’s core economies.","France is currently at the center of this turbulence. The country’s ten-year government bond yields recently reached 4.9 to 4.96 percent, climbing to levels unseen since July 2002. Long-term financing has grown drastically more expensive: Paris now faces annual interest rates between 5.33 and 5.5 percent on thirty-year bonds, compared to just 0.7 to 0.9 percent half a decade ago. With annual growth lingering around 0.7 percent, a budget deficit sitting at 5 percent of gross domestic product, and national debt reaching 121 percent of output, France far exceeds the Maastricht Treaty limits. Widespread student protests and profound political paralysis ahead of the upcoming presidential election, scheduled for April 18, further cloud the outlook. The race to succeed Emmanuel Macron features figures such as far-right leader Marine Le Pen and radical left veteran Jean-Luc Mélenchon, whose proposals include price controls, lower retirement ages, higher taxes on wealth, and opposition to NATO.","Meanwhile, Germany, historically the fiscal benchmark of the continent, faces severe structural friction of its own. German ten-year yields have advanced to 3.4 percent and thirty-year yields to 3.82 percent, a stark reversal from the negative territory observed five years ago. Hit by rising Chinese competition in the automotive sector, prominent manufacturers like Volkswagen are shedding tens of thousands of workers. German annual growth is projected at merely 1.1 percent for this year and next, while stubborn inflation at 3.3 percent and a weakening euro heighten import costs. Berlin’s national debt is anticipated to rise toward 70 percent of GDP by 2030, driven by escalating public expenditure and climbing defense allocations.","Military spending represents a broader fiscal dilemma for the European Union. Pressured by sustained regional threats and explicit demands from Washington to shoulder self-defense burdens, EU members are raising collective defense budgets to an unprecedented 454 billion euros. This marks an 8.6 percent increase compared to 2025 and a 75 percent surge compared to 2021. For the 27 EU member states, defense allocations will reach 2.2 percent of GDP, with equipment procurement consuming 36 percent of these outlays.","The ripple effects vary across the bloc. Greece, once the symbol of European insolvency, paradoxically commands a ten-year borrowing yield of 4.4 percent, below France’s borrowing rate, alongside roughly 2 percent GDP growth, although unemployment and debt remain substantial. In Italy, ten-year yields stand near 4.64 percent with sluggish 1 percent growth, while Spain balances strong 2.1 to 2.4 percent growth against elevated borrowing rates and high joblessness. As sovereign debt burdens swell, European policymakers confront shrinking fiscal flexibility in an increasingly demanding global landscape."],"facts":[{"fact":"France's ten-year government bond yields reached between 4.9% and 4.96%, marking their highest level since July 2002.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"French thirty-year borrowing yields rose to 5.33%-5.5%, compared to 0.7%-0.9% five years ago.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"France's annual economic growth is 0.7%, its budget deficit stands at 5% of GDP, and its debt-to-GDP ratio has reached 121%.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Germany's ten-year bond yields climbed to 3.4% and thirty-year yields rose to 3.82%, up from negative yields five years ago.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"European Union defense expenditure will reach a record 454 billion euros, an 8.6% rise over 2025 and a 75% increase compared to 2021.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Greece's ten-year government bond yield stands at 4.4% with near 2% GDP growth, while Italy's ten-year yield is 4.64% with roughly 1% growth.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"France's presidential election is scheduled for April 18, with candidates including Marine Le Pen and Jean-Luc Mélenchon aiming to succeed Emmanuel Macron.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"European","lemma":"European","pos":"adjective","he":"אירופי","name":false},{"text":"continent","lemma":"continent","pos":"noun","he":"יבשת","name":false},{"text":"celebrated","lemma":"celebrate","pos":"verb","he":"נודע, מהולל","name":false},{"t